export const P6={title:'Elimination and urinary catheter maintenance (Skill IV)',hook:'Indwelling catheters are a leading cause of healthcare-associated infection. Good catheter maintenance—perineal care, cleaning the tubing away from the body, securing the catheter and keeping the bag below the bladder—prevents CAUTI and is HOSA Skill IV.',objectives:['Assist with urinary and bowel elimination and recognize abnormal findings.','Measure intake and output and collect urine and stool specimens.','Prevent catheter-associated urinary tract infections.','Perform urinary catheter maintenance following HOSA Skill IV.'],sections:[
['Urinary elimination',[
'Adults usually produce about 1,200–1,500 mL of urine a day; output below about **30 mL per hour** is often reported. Normal urine is pale yellow to amber and clear. Report **dark, concentrated urine** (dehydration), **cloudy or foul-smelling urine** (possible infection), **blood**, pain or burning, frequency, urgency, **retention** (inability to empty the bladder) and **incontinence**. Assist patients to toilet on a schedule, provide privacy, offer bedpans, urinals and bedside commodes, and give **perineal care**—front to back for females, from the urethral opening outward for males. Encourage fluids unless restricted.',
'Intake includes all liquids and foods liquid at room temperature; output includes urine, emesis, liquid stool and drainage, measured in a **graduate** at eye level. **1 ounce = 30 mL**. A patient who has not voided for 8 hours or has a distended bladder is reported to the nurse.',
sort('Normal or report to the nurse?',['Usually normal','Report'],[['Pale yellow, clear urine',0],['Output of 15 mL per hour for three hours',1],['Cloudy, foul-smelling urine',1],['Voiding every 3–4 hours',0],['Blood in the urine',1],['No urine for 8 hours with a distended bladder',1]])]],
['Bowel elimination and specimens',[
'Normal bowel patterns vary; report **constipation** (hard, infrequent stools), **diarrhea**, **black tarry stools** (possible upper GI bleeding), **bright red blood**, abdominal pain or distension and **fecal impaction**. Fiber, fluids, activity and a regular toileting time support bowel health. Ostomy care includes emptying pouches when one-third to one-half full, protecting the skin around the stoma and reporting a stoma that is pale, dark or swollen.',
'**Specimen collection**: a **routine urine** specimen in a clean container; a **clean-catch midstream** specimen in a sterile container after cleansing, for culture; a **24-hour** collection (discard the first void, collect all urine for 24 hours, keep on ice or refrigerated as directed); a **catheter specimen** from the sampling port with a sterile syringe after cleaning the port—never from the drainage bag; **stool** specimens with a tongue blade, free of urine and paper; and **sputum** specimens coughed from deep in the lungs early in the morning. Label at the bedside with two identifiers, date, time and initials.',
match('Match the specimen to its method',[['Clean-catch urine','Cleanse, start voiding, collect midstream in a sterile cup'],['Catheter specimen','From the sampling port with a sterile syringe'],['24-hour urine','Discard first void, then collect all urine for 24 hours'],['Stool specimen','Use a tongue blade; no urine or paper'],['Sputum specimen','Deep cough early in the morning'],['Routine urine','Clean container any time']])]],
['Preventing CAUTI',[
'Indwelling (Foley) catheters drain urine continuously through a closed system. Prevent **catheter-associated urinary tract infections (CAUTIs)** by: keeping the system **closed** (never disconnecting tubing from the bag), keeping the **drainage bag below the level of the bladder and off the floor**, avoiding kinks and dependent loops, **securing the catheter to the thigh** to prevent tension and urethral trauma, providing **perineal and catheter care** daily and as needed, emptying the bag regularly using a separate clean container for each patient without letting the spout touch it, and prompting the team to **remove catheters as soon as they are no longer needed**.',
'Report signs of infection or problems: fever, cloudy or foul urine, blood, pain or burning, leaking around the catheter, decreased output, a kinked or occluded tube or redness and discharge at the meatus.',
sort('Prevents CAUTI or increases risk?',['Prevents CAUTI','Increases risk'],[['Bag below the bladder and off the floor',0],['Catheter secured to the thigh',0],['Disconnecting the tubing to empty the bag',1],['Laying the bag on the floor',1],['Cleaning the tubing away from the body',0],['Leaving the catheter in “just in case”',1]])]],
['Urinary catheter maintenance (Skill IV)',[
'In Skill IV (5 minutes), the competitor assembles supplies, performs hand hygiene, greets the patient and identifies self, **identifies the patient by ID bracelet and stated name and birthdate**, **explains the procedure and obtains consent**, provides privacy and applies gloves. Positioning: a **female** patient in the **dorsal recumbent** position; a **male** patient **supine with legs extended and thighs slightly abducted**. The competitor places a **waterproof pad** under the patient, performs **perineal care with soap and water** (or the agent specified), **cleans the catheter tubing moving away from the body**, and **evaluates, observes and verbalizes to the judge** any changes or discomfort—**discharge, how long the catheter has been in place, inflammation, edema, changes in tissue color and burning**. They **secure the catheter to the patient’s leg** and the **collection device to the bed frame below the bladder**, remove gloves, perform hand hygiene and communicate appropriately with standard precautions (30 points; 70% is 21).',
order('Urinary catheter maintenance (HOSA Skill IV)',['Hand hygiene; greet and identify with two identifiers','Explain, obtain consent and provide privacy; apply gloves','Position: female dorsal recumbent; male supine, thighs slightly abducted','Place a waterproof pad','Perform perineal care with soap and water','Clean the catheter tubing moving away from the body','Observe and report discharge, inflammation, edema, color change, burning','Secure the catheter to the leg; hang the bag on the bed frame below the bladder','Remove gloves, hand hygiene'])]],
['Applying the skill',[
kase('A catheter concern',{a:{text:'While cleaning the catheter, you notice redness and thick yellow discharge at the meatus, and the patient says it burns. What should you do?',choices:[['Complete care gently, then report the findings to the nurse and document','b','Correct. These may be signs of infection.',true],['Ignore it because catheters always irritate','b','Signs of infection must be reported.',false]]},b:{text:'You notice the drainage bag hanging on the side rail at the level of the bed. What do you do?',choices:[['Move it to the bed frame below the bladder','c','Correct. Rails move and the bag must stay below the bladder.',true],['Leave it—the height does not matter','c','Bags above the bladder allow backflow.',false]]},c:{text:'The nurse assesses the patient, obtains a urine specimen from the sampling port and contacts the provider.',end:true}})]],
["Bladder scanning and continence care",["Many units use a **portable bladder scanner**, an ultrasound device that estimates the volume of urine in the bladder noninvasively. It helps the team decide whether a patient with retention needs catheterization and supports removing catheters sooner. Typical steps: identify the patient, explain, position supine, apply gel above the pubic bone, aim the scan head toward the bladder, read the volume, clean the probe and report the result to the nurse.","**Incontinence** care protects skin and dignity: answer call lights quickly, offer **scheduled toileting** (prompted voiding every 2–3 hours), clean and dry the skin promptly with pH-balanced cleansers, apply barrier creams as directed and use absorbent products rather than leaving patients in wet linens. Incontinence-associated dermatitis and pressure injuries can develop quickly. External catheters are sometimes used to avoid indwelling catheters.",{"t":"check","q":"What is an advantage of using a bladder scanner?","options":["It estimates bladder volume without inserting a catheter","It removes urine from the bladder","It diagnoses kidney stones","It replaces intake and output records"],"why":"Noninvasive scanning helps avoid unnecessary catheterization."}]]
],takeaways:['Report output below about 30 mL/hour, dark, cloudy or bloody urine and no voiding for 8 hours; 1 oz = 30 mL.','Prevent CAUTI: closed system, bag below the bladder and off the floor, catheter secured to the thigh, clean tubing away from the body, prompt removal.','Skill IV: two identifiers, consent, privacy; female dorsal recumbent, male supine with thighs slightly abducted; pad, perineal care, clean tubing away, observe and verbalize findings, secure catheter and bag to the bed frame.'],terms:[['CAUTI','Catheter-associated urinary tract infection.'],['Dorsal recumbent','Lying on the back with knees bent and feet flat.'],['Closed drainage system','Catheter system that is never disconnected.']],mcqs:[q('In Skill IV, a female patient is positioned:','Dorsal recumbent','Prone','Sims’ position','Standing','Males are supine with thighs slightly abducted.'),q('Catheter tubing is cleaned:','Moving away from the body','Toward the body','In circles at the meatus only','It is not cleaned','Prevents introducing bacteria.'),q('The drainage bag is secured to the:','Bed frame below the bladder','Side rail','Floor','IV pole above the bed','Rails move and could raise the bag.'),q('A catheter urine specimen should be taken from:','The sampling port with a sterile syringe','The drainage bag','The tubing after disconnecting','The floor','Never from the bag.'),q('Which is a CAUTI prevention practice?','Keeping the system closed','Disconnecting the tubing to empty','Placing the bag on the floor','Leaving catheters in longer','Remove when no longer needed.'),q('Adult urine output below about how much per hour is often reported?','30 mL','300 mL','5 mL','100 mL','Low output may signal dehydration or kidney problems.'),q('Black, tarry stools may indicate:','Upper gastrointestinal bleeding','Normal digestion','A urinary tract infection','Dehydration only','Report immediately.'),q('In Skill IV, which finding is verbalized to the judge?','Discharge, inflammation, edema, color change or burning','The patient’s favorite food','The room number','Nothing','Observation is scored.')],frq:{prompt:'Describe how you would perform urinary catheter maintenance following HOSA Skill IV and explain how each step helps prevent catheter-associated urinary tract infection.',rubric:[['Preparation and consent',['identif','birthdate','explain','consent','privacy','gloves']],['Positioning and cleaning',['dorsal recumbent','supine','abduct','pad','perineal care','away from the body']],['Observation and reporting',['discharge','inflammation','edema','color','burning','verbaliz']],['Securing and CAUTI prevention',['secure','leg','bed frame','below the bladder','closed','infection']]],model:'After hand hygiene, greeting and identifying the patient with the ID bracelet and stated name and birthdate, I would explain the procedure, obtain consent, provide privacy and put on gloves. I would position a female patient dorsal recumbent or a male patient supine with legs extended and thighs slightly abducted and place a waterproof pad. I would perform perineal care with soap and water, then clean the catheter tubing moving away from the body so that bacteria are carried away from the urethra rather than toward the bladder. I would observe and verbalize any discharge, how long the catheter has been in place, inflammation, edema, color change or burning, because these may signal infection or trauma and must be reported to the nurse. Finally I would secure the catheter to the patient’s leg to prevent tension and urethral injury and hang the collection bag on the bed frame below the bladder so urine cannot flow back, keeping the system closed and off the floor, then remove gloves and perform hand hygiene. Together these steps reduce the bacteria and backflow that cause CAUTI.'},sources:[RULES,CDC_CAUTI,MEDLINE_IO]};

export const P7={title:'IV therapy basics and discontinuing a peripheral IV (Skill I)',hook:'Most hospitalized patients have a peripheral IV catheter. When the order comes to remove it, the PCT must stop the infusion, remove the dressing without dislodging the catheter, withdraw it smoothly, hold pressure and check that the whole catheter came out—every step scored in Skill I.',objectives:['Describe peripheral IV therapy, common complications and the PCT’s role.','Recognize infiltration, phlebitis and other IV site problems.','Discontinue a peripheral IV following HOSA Skill I.','Document IV removal and teach site care.'],sections:[
['Peripheral IV therapy',[
'A **peripheral intravenous (PIV) catheter** is a short, flexible tube placed in a vein, usually in the hand or forearm, to give fluids, medications and blood products. Catheters are sized by **gauge**—a smaller number is a larger catheter (for example, 18-gauge for blood and rapid fluids, 20–22 gauge for routine use, 24-gauge for small veins). The site is covered with a **transparent semipermeable membrane (TSM) dressing** so it can be inspected. Infusions run by gravity or through an **infusion pump**, and **roller clamps** start and stop flow. Nurses start, monitor and manage IV therapy; PCTs commonly observe sites, report problems and, where policy allows, **discontinue** peripheral IVs after an order.',
'PCTs never adjust infusion rates, give IV medications or reinsert catheters. If a pump alarms, the PCT notifies the nurse rather than silencing and ignoring it.',
flip('IV basics',[['Gauge','Smaller number = larger catheter'],['TSM dressing','Clear dressing allowing site inspection'],['Roller clamp','Starts and stops gravity flow'],['Infusion pump','Controls the rate; alarms go to the nurse'],['Catheter hub','The part outside the skin that connects to tubing'],['Peripheral IV','Short catheter in a hand or arm vein']])]],
['IV site complications',[
'**Infiltration** is leakage of fluid into surrounding tissue: the site is **swollen, cool, pale and taut**, and the infusion may slow. **Extravasation** is infiltration of a medication that damages tissue. **Phlebitis** is inflammation of the vein: **redness, warmth, tenderness** and sometimes a red streak or hard, cordlike vein. Other complications include **infection** (drainage, fever), **occlusion** (no flow), **hematoma** and **air embolism**. **Fluid overload** shows as shortness of breath, crackles, swelling and rising blood pressure. PCTs report any of these immediately to the nurse; the IV is usually stopped and removed by the team.',
sort('Infiltration or phlebitis?',['Infiltration','Phlebitis'],[['Cool, pale, swollen site',0],['Infusion slows or stops',0],['Red streak along the vein',1],['Warm, tender, red site',1],['Taut, puffy skin around the catheter',0],['Hard, cordlike vein',1]])]],
['Preparing to discontinue an IV',[
'Before removing a peripheral IV, the competitor **verifies the physician’s order** and **reviews the scenario for any bleeding issues**—patients on anticoagulants or with low platelets need longer pressure. They obtain supplies (gloves, dry sterile gauze, tape, a waste container), perform hand hygiene, **put on gloves**, **identify the patient** by ID bracelet and stated name and birthdate, identify themselves, explain the skill and provide privacy. The roller clamp is turned off before any dressing is lifted so fluid does not leak.',
chk('Why review the patient for bleeding issues before removing an IV?',['Patients on anticoagulants or with low platelets need longer pressure to stop bleeding','It is not necessary','To decide the IV gauge','To choose the dressing color'],'Bleeding risk changes how long pressure is held.')]],
['Discontinuing a peripheral IV (Skill I)',[
'In Skill I (5 minutes), after preparation, the competitor removes the dressing carefully: **removes overlying tape**; removes a **transparent membrane dressing by lifting one corner and pulling the side laterally while holding the catheter hub**, then **repeating from the other side**—or, for a gauze dressing, removing **one layer at a time**—leaving the tape that secures the catheter to the skin intact until the catheter is stabilized. They **turn the IV tubing roller clamp off** and remove tape securing the tubing, then remove the remaining dressing and tape **while stabilizing the catheter**. Holding **dry gauze over the site**, they **apply light pressure and withdraw the catheter with a slow, steady movement**, keeping the **hub parallel to the skin**. They **apply pressure with dry sterile gauze for 2 to 3 minutes** (longer if bleeding risk) and **secure tape snugly over the gauze**.',
'Next they **inspect the catheter for intactness, noting tip integrity and length**—a missing tip is an emergency to report immediately. They discard supplies, remove gloves and perform hand hygiene, **instruct the patient to report any redness (erythema), pain, drainage or swelling** after removal and **document the time the IV was discontinued, the condition of the site and the gauge and length of the catheter**, communicating appropriately with standard precautions throughout (51 points; 70% is 35.7).',
order('Discontinue a peripheral IV (HOSA Skill I)',['Verify the order and review bleeding risk; gather supplies','Hand hygiene, gloves, identify with two identifiers, explain, privacy','Remove tape and the membrane dressing laterally while holding the hub','Turn the roller clamp off; remove tubing tape','Stabilize the catheter while removing remaining dressing','Hold dry gauze over the site and withdraw slowly, hub parallel to skin','Apply pressure 2–3 minutes; tape gauze snugly','Inspect the catheter tip and length; teach the patient; document'])]],
['Oxygen and wound basics',[
'PCTs also support patients receiving **oxygen** by nasal cannula (typically 1–6 L/min), simple mask or non-rebreather mask. They keep tubing free of kinks, check skin behind the ears and nose for pressure, ensure no smoking or open flames and **never change the flow rate**, reporting low oxygen saturation or breathing difficulty. For **wounds**, PCTs observe and report drainage (serous, sanguineous, serosanguineous, purulent), redness, swelling and odor, reinforce dressings as directed and assist the nurse with dressing changes according to scope.',
kase('After removal',{a:{text:'You remove a 20-gauge IV, hold pressure for 3 minutes and inspect the catheter. The tip looks jagged and shorter than expected. What should you do?',choices:[['Keep the catheter, notify the nurse immediately and monitor the patient','b','Correct. A missing fragment can travel in the vein—an emergency.',true],['Throw it away and document normally','b','The catheter must be kept and reported.',false]]},b:{text:'The patient takes an anticoagulant and the site is still oozing after 3 minutes. What next?',choices:[['Continue firm pressure until bleeding stops, then apply a pressure dressing and report','c','Correct. Anticoagulated patients need longer pressure.',true],['Remove the gauze to check every 10 seconds','c','Frequent lifting disrupts clot formation.',false]]},c:{text:'Bleeding stops after 6 minutes. You document the time of removal, site condition, catheter gauge and length and the findings reported.',end:true}})]],
["Supporting transfusions and fluid balance",["PCTs often help monitor patients receiving **blood transfusions**. Nurses verify the blood product with a second qualified person and start the transfusion; PCTs may be asked to measure **baseline vital signs** and repeat them at set times (commonly after 15 minutes and as policy directs). Reactions occur most often in the first 15 minutes: report **fever, chills, itching or hives, back pain, shortness of breath, chest pain, dark urine or a drop in blood pressure** immediately, and the nurse stops the transfusion.","For all IV patients, accurate **intake and output**, **daily weights** and observation for **fluid overload** (swelling, crackles, shortness of breath) or **dehydration** (dry mouth, dark urine, dizziness) help the nurse manage fluids. Keep IV tubing free of kinks and protect the site during bathing and transfers; do not let IV bags hang below the site or tubing dangle on the floor.",{"t":"w","kind":"sort","title":"Possible transfusion reaction or expected?","buckets":["Possible reaction—report now","Usually expected"],"items":[["Chills and fever 10 minutes after starting",0],["Hives and itching",0],["Back pain and dark urine",0],["Stable vital signs and comfort",1],["Shortness of breath",0],["Patient reading quietly",1]]}]]
],takeaways:['Peripheral IVs give fluids and medications; smaller gauge numbers are larger catheters; PCTs observe and report but do not adjust rates or give IV medications.','Infiltration: cool, pale, swollen, slowed flow; phlebitis: red, warm, tender, cordlike vein—report both.','Skill I: verify order and bleeding risk, remove dressing laterally while holding the hub, clamp off, withdraw slowly with hub parallel, pressure 2–3 minutes, inspect tip and length, teach and document time, site and catheter gauge/length.'],terms:[['Infiltration','Leakage of IV fluid into surrounding tissue.'],['Phlebitis','Inflammation of a vein.'],['Gauge','Measure of catheter or needle diameter.']],mcqs:[q('What is the first step of Skill I?','Verify the physician’s order and review bleeding issues','Pull the catheter out','Turn up the infusion','Apply a bandage','Safety checks come first.'),q('A transparent dressing is removed by:','Lifting a corner and pulling laterally while holding the hub','Ripping it off upward quickly','Cutting it with scissors over the catheter','Soaking it in alcohol','Avoid dislodging the catheter.'),q('When withdrawing the catheter, the hub is kept:','Parallel to the skin','Pointing straight up','At a 90° angle','Twisted','Slow, steady movement.'),q('How long is pressure applied after removal in Skill I?','2 to 3 minutes','10 seconds','15 minutes','No pressure','Longer if bleeding risk.'),q('After removal, the catheter is inspected for:','Tip integrity and length','Color only','Brand name','Temperature','A missing tip is an emergency.'),q('A cool, pale, swollen IV site suggests:','Infiltration','Phlebitis','Normal function','Dehydration','Report to the nurse.'),q('Which is documented after IV removal in Skill I?','Time discontinued, site condition and catheter gauge and length','Only the patient’s name','The patient’s diet','The IV bag brand','These are rating-sheet items.'),q('A PCT notices an infusion pump alarming. The PCT should:','Notify the nurse','Change the rate','Turn off the pump permanently','Ignore it','PCTs do not manage infusions.')],frq:{prompt:'Describe how you would discontinue a peripheral IV following HOSA Skill I for a patient taking an anticoagulant, including preparation, technique, inspection, teaching and documentation.',rubric:[['Preparation',['order','bleeding','anticoagulant','gloves','identif','explain','privacy']],['Removal technique',['tape','lateral','hub','roller clamp','stabiliz','slow','parallel']],['Pressure and inspection',['pressure','2','3 minutes','longer','gauze','tip','length']],['Teaching and documentation',['erythema','pain','drainage','edema','time','site','gauge','report']]],model:'I would verify the physician’s order and note that the patient takes an anticoagulant, gather gloves, dry sterile gauze and tape, perform hand hygiene, put on gloves, identify the patient by the ID bracelet and stated name and birthdate, explain the procedure and provide privacy. I would remove the overlying tape, lift one corner of the transparent dressing and pull it laterally while holding the catheter hub, repeat from the other side, turn the roller clamp off and remove the tubing tape, then remove the remaining dressing while stabilizing the catheter. Holding dry gauze over the site, I would withdraw the catheter with a slow, steady motion, keeping the hub parallel to the skin, and apply pressure for at least 2–3 minutes—longer because of the anticoagulant—until bleeding stops, then tape the gauze snugly. I would inspect the catheter for an intact tip and full length and report immediately if it were not intact. After discarding supplies, removing gloves and performing hand hygiene, I would teach the patient to report redness, pain, drainage or swelling, and document the time of removal, site condition and catheter gauge and length, reporting any prolonged bleeding to the nurse.'},sources:[RULES,MEDLINE_IV,INS]};

export const P8={title:'Phlebotomy: routine ETS venipuncture (Skill V)',hook:'Skill V is the longest PCT skill—46 steps worth 68 points—and phlebotomy is 14% of the test. A successful draw depends on correct identification, the right vein, the right tubes in the right order and a tourniquet that never stays on longer than two minutes.',objectives:['Select appropriate veins and equipment for venipuncture.','Apply the order of draw and tube additive knowledge.','Perform routine evacuated tube system (ETS) venipuncture following HOSA Skill V.','Prevent and manage complications and pre-analytical errors.'],sections:[
['Equipment and veins',[
'The **evacuated tube system (ETS)** uses a **double-pointed multisample needle** (commonly 21-gauge, or 22-gauge for smaller veins), a **tube holder (Vacutainer holder)** and **vacuum tubes** whose stopper colors identify additives. Other supplies include a **tourniquet**, 70% isopropyl alcohol wipes, gauze, hypoallergenic tape or bandage, labels, gloves and a **sharps container**. A **butterfly (winged infusion set)** is used for small or fragile veins.',
'The preferred site is the **antecubital area**: the **median cubital vein** first (large, well anchored and less painful), then the **cephalic vein** on the thumb side and the **basilic vein** on the little-finger side (near the brachial artery and median nerve—used last). Avoid arms with IV infusions, fistulas, mastectomy on that side (unless permitted), hematomas, burns or scars. Palpate with the **tip of the index finger** to trace the vein’s path and depth; a good vein feels bouncy.',
match('Match the vein or item to its description',[['Median cubital vein','First-choice antecubital vein'],['Cephalic vein','Thumb side of the antecubital area'],['Basilic vein','Little-finger side; near nerve and artery—use last'],['Butterfly set','For small or fragile veins'],['Multisample needle','Double-pointed needle for ETS'],['Tube holder','Connects the needle and tubes']])]],
['Order of draw and additives',[
'The **CLSI order of draw** prevents additive carryover between tubes: **1. blood cultures (sterile)**, **2. light blue** (sodium citrate—coagulation tests such as PT/INR; must be filled completely), **3. red or gold/tiger-top** (serum; clot activator, with or without gel), **4. green** (heparin—plasma chemistry), **5. lavender or purple** (EDTA—hematology such as a CBC), **6. gray** (sodium fluoride/potassium oxalate—glucose and lactate). Tubes with additives are **gently inverted** the number of times the manufacturer specifies (often 3–4 for citrate, 5–10 for others); never shake. Tubes are filled to their **maximum vacuum capacity** for the correct blood-to-additive ratio.',
order('Order of draw',['Blood culture bottles','Light blue: sodium citrate','Red or gold: serum tubes','Green: heparin','Lavender: EDTA','Gray: sodium fluoride/potassium oxalate']),
match('Match the tube to its common use',[['Light blue','Coagulation (PT/INR)'],['Lavender','Complete blood count'],['Gray','Glucose'],['Green','Plasma chemistry'],['Gold or red','Serum chemistry'],['Blood culture bottles','Detecting bloodstream infection']])]],
['Preparing the patient and site (Skill V, part 1)',[
'In Skill V (10 minutes), the competitor **reviews the requisition**, assembles supplies, greets the patient and identifies self, **identifies the patient** by ID bracelet and stated name and birthdate, **verbally matches the identification to the requisition**, **explains the procedure and obtains consent**, sanitizes hands and puts on gloves. They **attach the needle firmly to the holder with the cover still on**, **apply the tourniquet 3–4 inches above the venipuncture site** (snug but not stopping arterial flow), have the patient **make a fist**, **select the vein in the antecubital area by palpating with the tip of the index finger** to trace its path and depth, then **release the tourniquet and have the patient open the fist**. They **clean the site with 70% isopropyl alcohol in a circular motion from the center outward** and **let it air dry for up to 60 seconds**, then **reapply the tourniquet** and have the patient make a fist again.',
'The tourniquet must **not remain on the arm for more than 120 seconds** during site selection—prolonged tourniquet time causes **hemoconcentration**, altering results such as potassium, protein and cell counts.',
chk('Why is the tourniquet released after selecting the vein and before cleaning?',['To avoid leaving it on more than about 2 minutes, which causes hemoconcentration','To let the alcohol dry faster','Because the patient asked','To change tubes'],'Reapply it just before the puncture.')]],
['Performing the draw (Skill V, part 2)',[
'The competitor **holds the assembly in the dominant hand, thumb on top and fingers underneath**, places the **first tube in the holder without pushing it onto the back needle**, removes the needle sheath and **anchors the vein with the thumb of the nondominant hand, stretching the skin downward below the site**. With the **bevel up**, aligned parallel to the vein, they **insert the needle at a 15–30 degree angle in a quick, smooth motion**, then place **two fingers on the holder’s flanges** and use the thumb to **push the tube onto the needle**, keeping the needle still in the vein. When blood flows, the patient **releases the fist**. Each tube **fills to maximum capacity**, is removed by curling the fingers underneath and pushing on the holder with the thumb, and is **gently inverted**; remaining tubes are filled in order. **As the last tube fills, the tourniquet is released**; the last tube is removed, **gauze is placed over the site**, the **needle is removed quickly and the safety device engaged**, and the **entire unit goes into the sharps container**.',
'The patient applies **pressure without bending the elbow** until bleeding stops. Before leaving the patient, the competitor **labels the tubes**, checks for bleeding or **hematoma**, applies a **hypoallergenic bandage**, disinfects the work area, disposes of materials, removes gloves, performs hand hygiene, **completes the requisition and documents**, verbalizes routing the specimen to the appropriate place and communicates appropriately (68 points; 70% is 47.6).',
order('Routine ETS venipuncture, key steps (HOSA Skill V)',['Review requisition; identify patient and match to requisition; consent','Gloves; attach needle to holder; tourniquet 3–4 inches above site; fist','Palpate the antecubital vein; release tourniquet; open fist','Clean center outward with 70% alcohol; air dry','Reapply tourniquet; fist; anchor vein below the site','Insert bevel up at 15–30° in one smooth motion','Fill tubes in order to capacity; invert gently; release fist','Release tourniquet as last tube fills; gauze, withdraw, engage safety device','Sharps container; pressure without bending elbow; label at bedside; bandage; document'])]],
['Complications and pre-analytical errors',[
'**Hematoma** (blood under the skin) results from needle passing through the vein, inadequate pressure or bending the elbow; stop, remove the tourniquet and needle and apply pressure. **Syncope** (fainting): stop the draw, remove the needle, lower the patient’s head or have them lie down and call for help. **Nerve injury** causes sharp, shooting pain—remove the needle immediately. **Arterial puncture** produces bright red, pulsing blood; apply pressure for at least 5 minutes. **Hemolysis** (ruptured red cells) results from small needles, vigorous mixing, pulling hard on a syringe or alcohol not dried. Other pre-analytical errors include wrong patient identification, wrong tube, wrong order of draw, underfilled light blue tubes, prolonged tourniquet time and drawing above an IV site.',
kase('A patient feels faint',{a:{text:'Partway through the draw, the patient says she feels lightheaded and looks pale. What do you do first?',choices:[['Release the tourniquet, remove the needle, apply pressure and lower her head or help her lie down','b','Correct. Stop the procedure and protect the patient from falling.',true],['Keep filling tubes quickly','b','The patient could faint and fall.',false]]},b:{text:'She recovers after a few minutes. What should be documented?',choices:[['The syncopal episode, actions taken and that the nurse was notified','c','Correct. Document and report.',true],['Nothing—she is fine now','c','Adverse events must be documented.',false]]},c:{text:'The draw is completed later with the patient lying down, and the event is documented.',end:true}})]]
],takeaways:['Preferred vein: median cubital, then cephalic, basilic last; avoid IV arms, fistulas and hematomas.','Order of draw: blood cultures, light blue, red/gold, green, lavender, gray; fill to capacity and invert gently.','Skill V: tourniquet 3–4 in above the site, never more than 120 seconds; clean center outward and air dry; anchor below; bevel up 15–30°; release tourniquet as the last tube fills; engage safety device; label at bedside.'],terms:[['Order of draw','Tube sequence that prevents additive carryover.'],['Hemoconcentration','Increase in cell and analyte concentration from prolonged tourniquet use.'],['Hemolysis','Rupture of red blood cells that alters results.']],mcqs:[q('Where is the tourniquet applied in Skill V?','3–4 inches above the venipuncture site','At the wrist','Directly over the vein','At the shoulder','Snug but not stopping arterial flow.'),q('The needle is inserted at what angle?','15–30 degrees, bevel up','90 degrees','45 degrees, bevel down','5 degrees','Quick, smooth motion.'),q('Which tube is drawn first after blood cultures?','Light blue','Lavender','Gray','Green','It must be filled completely.'),q('A lavender tube contains:','EDTA','Sodium citrate','Heparin','Sodium fluoride','Used for CBCs.'),q('The tourniquet should not stay on longer than:','120 seconds','5 minutes','10 seconds','30 minutes','Prevents hemoconcentration.'),q('When is the tourniquet released at the end?','As the last tube is filling','Before inserting the needle','After withdrawing the needle','It is never released','Then remove the tube and needle.'),q('How is the site cleaned?','70% isopropyl alcohol in circles from the center outward','Iodine in a back-and-forth motion','Water only','From the outside in','Let it air dry.'),q('The patient should apply pressure without:','Bending the elbow','Breathing','Sitting','Using gauze','Bending increases hematoma risk.')],frq:{prompt:'Explain how to perform a routine ETS venipuncture following HOSA Skill V for a CBC, PT/INR and glucose, including identification, the order of draw and how you would prevent complications.',rubric:[['Identification and preparation',['requisition','identif','birthdate','match','consent','gloves']],['Site selection and cleaning',['tourniquet','3','4 inches','median cubital','palpat','release','alcohol','center outward','air dry']],['Draw technique and order',['bevel up','15','30','light blue','lavender','gray','invert','fill']],['Completion and complications',['release the tourniquet','safety','sharps','label','hematoma','120 seconds','syncope']]],model:'I would review the requisition, gather supplies, greet the patient, identify them by the ID bracelet and stated name and birthdate, match this to the requisition, explain and obtain consent, sanitize my hands and put on gloves. I would attach the needle to the holder, apply the tourniquet 3–4 inches above the antecubital area, have the patient make a fist, palpate the median cubital vein with my index fingertip, then release the tourniquet within two minutes, clean the site with 70% alcohol from the center outward and let it air dry. After reapplying the tourniquet, I would anchor the vein below the site, insert the needle bevel up at 15–30 degrees and fill the tubes in the correct order—light blue for PT/INR (filled completely), lavender for the CBC and gray for glucose—inverting each gently. I would release the tourniquet as the last tube fills, remove the tube, place gauze, withdraw the needle and engage the safety device, dispose of the unit in the sharps container and have the patient hold pressure without bending the elbow. I would label tubes at the bedside, check for a hematoma, apply a bandage, document and route the specimens. To prevent complications I would avoid prolonged tourniquet time, use proper angle and anchoring, watch for fainting and stop immediately for nerve pain or a hematoma.'},sources:[RULES,CLSI,MEDLINE_VENI,OSHA_BBP]};

export const P9={title:'Point-of-care glucose testing and diabetes care (Skill VII)',hook:'A blood glucose result at the bedside can prompt an insulin dose or treatment for a dangerous low within minutes. Point-of-care glucose testing—Skill VII—combines capillary puncture, quality control, infection prevention and clear reporting.',objectives:['Explain diabetes, hypoglycemia and hyperglycemia and their signs.','Perform capillary puncture correctly and avoid pre-analytical errors.','Perform glucometer testing following HOSA Skill VII.','Apply CLIA, quality control and infection-prevention requirements for point-of-care testing.'],sections:[
['Diabetes basics',[
'**Diabetes mellitus** is a group of conditions in which blood glucose is too high because the body does not make enough **insulin** (type 1), does not use it effectively (type 2) or both; **gestational diabetes** develops in pregnancy. Normal fasting glucose is generally **70–99 mg/dL**; facilities set ranges for reporting. **Hypoglycemia** (often below 70 mg/dL) causes **shakiness, sweating, rapid heartbeat, confusion, irritability, hunger** and, if severe, seizures or unconsciousness; it requires prompt treatment by the nurse, often with **15 grams of fast-acting carbohydrate** for a conscious patient. **Hyperglycemia** causes thirst, frequent urination, fatigue and blurred vision; very high values with ketones (DKA) are emergencies.',
'PCTs check glucose on schedule (often before meals and at bedtime), deliver meals on time for patients receiving insulin, observe for signs of highs and lows and report results promptly. Foot care, skin checks and accurate intake records also support diabetes care.',
{t:'w',kind:'glucose',title:'Glucose and insulin',prompt:'Compare a healthy body with type 1 and type 2 diabetes after a meal.'},
sort('Hypoglycemia or hyperglycemia?',['Hypoglycemia','Hyperglycemia'],[['Shaky and sweaty',0],['Confusion before lunch after insulin',0],['Extreme thirst',1],['Frequent urination',1],['Cool, clammy skin',0],['Fruity breath with deep breathing',1]])]],
['Capillary puncture',[
'**Capillary (skin) puncture** collects a small blood sample from the fingertip (adults and older children) or the **heel** (infants under about 1 year, using the medial or lateral plantar surface). For fingersticks, use the **side of the fleshy pad of the middle or ring finger**, perpendicular to the fingerprint lines, avoiding the center of the pad, cold, swollen, scarred or infected sites and the side of a mastectomy or IV. Warm the site if needed, **clean with 70% alcohol and let it dry** (wet alcohol causes hemolysis and inaccurate results), puncture with a **single-use safety lancet**, and **wipe away the first drop**, which contains tissue fluid and residual alcohol. Avoid **milking** or squeezing hard, which dilutes the sample with tissue fluid.',
match('Match the step to its reason',[['Let alcohol dry','Prevents hemolysis and false results'],['Wipe away the first drop','Removes tissue fluid and alcohol'],['Avoid squeezing hard','Prevents dilution with tissue fluid'],['Use the side of the finger pad','Fewer nerve endings and safer depth'],['Single-use lancet','Prevents bloodborne pathogen transmission'],['Warm a cold hand','Improves blood flow']])]],
['Glucometer testing (Skill VII)',[
'In Skill VII (8 minutes), the competitor **reviews the requisition**, assembles supplies, performs hand hygiene, greets and identifies self, **identifies the patient by ID bracelet and stated name and birthdate**, **verbally matches the identification to the requisition**, **explains and obtains consent** and puts on gloves. They **prepare the glucose monitor and confirm the patient ID** in the meter, **select, prepare and cleanse the site**, **perform the capillary puncture and wipe away the first drop**, **place the second drop on the test strip**, apply **new gauze** to the finger, **read the result and turn off the monitor**, then **clean the monitor and dispose of supplies**. After removing gloves and performing hand hygiene, they complete **environmental and patient safety checks**—**call bell within reach**, **patient position**, **belongings within reach**, **offer elimination** and **assess pain**—**reinforce patient education and document**, perform hand hygiene, **document findings in the Nurses’ Notes** and **communicate findings to the nurse** (45 points; 70% is 31.5).',
order('Glucometer test (HOSA Skill VII)',['Review requisition; hand hygiene; identify and match to requisition','Explain, consent and put on gloves','Prepare the meter and confirm patient ID','Select and cleanse the site; let it dry','Puncture and wipe away the first drop','Apply the second drop to the strip','Gauze on the finger; read the result; turn off the meter','Clean the meter, dispose of supplies, safety checks, document and report to the nurse'])]],
['Quality and infection prevention',[
'Glucometers are **CLIA-waived** devices, but accuracy still requires following the manufacturer’s instructions: run **quality control** with control solutions as scheduled, check strip **expiration dates** and storage conditions, confirm the meter is coded or calibrated if required and document QC. Results outside the meter’s range or inconsistent with the patient’s condition are reported and often confirmed with a laboratory test.',
'Shared glucometers have transmitted **hepatitis B** between patients when not cleaned properly. CDC guidance: use **single-use, auto-disabling lancets**, never share lancing devices or fingerstick pens between patients, and **clean and disinfect the meter after every use** according to the manufacturer. Change gloves and perform hand hygiene between patients.',
kase('A surprising reading',{a:{text:'A patient who just ate lunch has a glucometer reading of 42 mg/dL but is alert and talking normally. What should you do?',choices:[['Report immediately to the nurse; the result may be inaccurate and needs confirmation, and the patient should be assessed','b','Correct. Unexpected results must be reported and verified.',true],['Record it and move on','b','A critically low value requires immediate action.',false]]},b:{text:'You realize you did not let the alcohol dry before the puncture. What could that cause?',choices:[['Hemolysis and an inaccurate result','c','Correct. Wet alcohol affects the sample.',true],['A more accurate result','c','It reduces accuracy.',false]]},c:{text:'The nurse rechecks with proper technique and gets 156 mg/dL. You document both results and the reason for the recheck.',end:true}})]],
['Patient teaching',[
'Many patients test glucose at home. Teaching points include washing hands with warm water, rotating fingertip sites, using a new lancet each time, applying an adequate drop, recording results with meal and insulin times, recognizing symptoms of highs and lows, and knowing when to call the provider. Reinforce what the nurse or diabetes educator has taught and report questions to the nurse.',
flip('Glucose testing essentials',[['Fasting normal','About 70–99 mg/dL'],['Hypoglycemia','Often below 70 mg/dL'],['First drop','Wiped away'],['Lancet','Single-use, never shared'],['Meter cleaning','After every patient'],['QC','Control solutions as scheduled']])]],
["Other point-of-care tests",["Besides glucose, PCTs may perform other **waived point-of-care tests** after training. **Urine reagent strips** measure glucose, protein, ketones, blood, nitrite, leukocytes, pH and specific gravity; strips are held by the clear end, dipped fully, tapped and read at timed intervals. **Urine pregnancy tests** detect hCG with a control line. **Fecal occult blood** tests detect hidden blood. **Rapid antigen tests** detect strep, influenza or COVID-19 from swabs. **Pulse oximetry** estimates oxygen saturation. Each test requires correct patient identification, following timing exactly, running quality control as required, and documenting and reporting results—PCTs do not interpret results for patients.",{"t":"w","kind":"order","title":"Universal steps for any point-of-care test","steps":["Verify the order and identify the patient with two identifiers","Check test kit expiration and quality control","Collect the specimen correctly","Follow the timing in the instructions exactly","Record the result and control line or QC","Report the result to the nurse and document"]}]]
],takeaways:['Hypoglycemia (often <70 mg/dL): shaky, sweaty, confused; hyperglycemia: thirst, frequent urination; report results promptly.','Capillary puncture: side of the middle or ring fingertip, alcohol dry, wipe the first drop, avoid squeezing; heel sticks for infants.','Skill VII: match ID to requisition, confirm ID in the meter, wipe first drop, second drop on the strip, clean meter, safety checks (call bell, position, belongings, elimination, pain), document and report to the nurse.'],terms:[['Point-of-care testing','Testing performed at or near the bedside.'],['Capillary puncture','Skin puncture to collect a small blood sample.'],['Quality control','Testing known controls to confirm accuracy.']],mcqs:[q('Which drop of blood is used for the glucose test in Skill VII?','The second drop','The first drop','Any drop','A drop mixed with alcohol','The first is wiped away.'),q('Why must alcohol dry before the puncture?','Wet alcohol can cause hemolysis and inaccurate results','It is not necessary','To keep the finger cold','To change the color of blood','Let it air dry.'),q('Which finger site is preferred for capillary puncture?','The side of the fleshy pad of the middle or ring finger','The center of the thumb','The fingernail','The index finger tip center','Fewer nerve endings.'),q('Glucometers shared between patients must be:','Cleaned and disinfected after every use','Used without cleaning','Shared with lancing pens','Stored in the patient room only','Prevents hepatitis B transmission.'),q('Which is part of the Skill VII safety checks?','Call bell within reach','Changing the IV rate','Ordering a diet','Interpreting the result','Also position, belongings, elimination and pain.'),q('A typical normal fasting glucose is about:','70–99 mg/dL','200–300 mg/dL','10–20 mg/dL','400 mg/dL','Facilities set reporting ranges.'),q('Squeezing the finger hard can:','Dilute the sample with tissue fluid','Improve accuracy','Lower the reading permanently','Prevent infection','Avoid milking.'),q('Glucometers are generally classified under CLIA as:','Waived tests','High-complexity tests','Not regulated','Research only','They still need QC.')],frq:{prompt:'Explain how to perform a bedside glucose test following HOSA Skill VII, including how you ensure an accurate result, prevent infection and complete the post-procedure checks.',rubric:[['Identification and preparation',['requisition','identif','match','consent','gloves','meter','confirm']],['Accurate technique',['site','alcohol','dry','first drop','second drop','squeez']],['Infection prevention and QC',['clean','disinfect','single-use','lancet','quality control','expiration']],['Safety checks and reporting',['call bell','position','belongings','elimination','pain','document','nurse']]],model:'I would review the requisition, gather supplies, perform hand hygiene, introduce myself, identify the patient by the ID bracelet and stated name and birthdate and match this to the requisition, explain the test, obtain consent and put on gloves. I would prepare the meter, confirm the patient ID in it and check that strips are unexpired and quality control is current. I would choose the side of the fleshy pad of the middle or ring finger, warm it if cold, clean it with alcohol and let it dry completely, puncture with a single-use safety lancet, wipe away the first drop and apply the second drop to the strip without squeezing hard. I would place new gauze on the finger, read and record the result and turn off the meter. To prevent infection, I would never reuse lancets and would clean and disinfect the meter after use, then dispose of supplies, remove gloves and perform hand hygiene. Finally I would complete safety checks—call bell within reach, patient positioned comfortably, belongings within reach, elimination offered and pain assessed—reinforce teaching, document in the Nurses’ Notes and report the result to the nurse, immediately if it is outside reporting limits.'},sources:[RULES,NIDDK_DM,CDC_GLUC,CMS_CLIA]};

export const P10={title:'Electrocardiography: obtaining a 12-lead EKG (Skill VI)',hook:'Where does V4 go? The fifth intercostal space at the midclavicular line. EKG is 10% of the test, and Skill VI is worth 60 points: correct electrode placement, a clean tracing and privacy throughout.',objectives:['Relate the heart’s conduction system to the EKG waveform.','Place the ten electrodes of a 12-lead EKG correctly.','Obtain a 12-lead EKG following HOSA Skill VI.','Recognize artifacts and rhythms that must be reported.'],sections:[
['The conduction system',[
'The heart’s electrical impulse begins in the **sinoatrial (SA) node**, the natural pacemaker, at about 60–100 beats per minute, spreads through the atria, pauses at the **atrioventricular (AV) node**, then travels through the **bundle of His**, the **right and left bundle branches** and the **Purkinje fibers** to the ventricles. On the EKG, the **P wave** represents atrial depolarization, the **PR interval** the time from atrial to ventricular activation, the **QRS complex** ventricular depolarization and the **T wave** ventricular repolarization. Standard paper runs at **25 mm/second**; each small box is **0.04 second** and each large box **0.20 second**.',
label('conduction','The conduction system',[['sa','SA node','Natural pacemaker in the right atrium.'],['av','AV node','Delays the impulse so the ventricles can fill.'],['his','Bundle of His','Carries the impulse into the septum.'],['rbb','Right bundle branch','Conducts to the right ventricle.'],['lbb','Left bundle branch','Conducts to the left ventricle.'],['purkinje','Purkinje fibers','Spread the impulse through the ventricles.']]),
label('ecg-wave','The EKG waveform',[['p','P wave','Atrial depolarization.'],['qrs','QRS complex','Ventricular depolarization.'],['t','T wave','Ventricular repolarization.'],['pr','PR interval','Atrial to ventricular conduction time.'],['st','ST segment','Elevated or depressed in heart muscle injury.'],['rr','R-R interval','Used to calculate heart rate.']])]],
['Electrode placement',[
'A **12-lead EKG** uses **10 electrodes** that create 12 views of the heart. **Limb leads**: **RA** (right arm), **LA** (left arm), **RL** (right leg, the ground) and **LL** (left leg), placed on the limbs avoiding bony areas. **Chest (precordial) leads**: **V1**—fourth intercostal space at the **right** sternal border; **V2**—fourth intercostal space at the **left** sternal border; **V4**—**fifth intercostal space at the left midclavicular line**; **V3**—**midway between V2 and V4**; **V5**—level with V4 at the **left anterior axillary line**; **V6**—level with V4 at the **left midaxillary line**. Place V4 before V3 so V3 can be centered. Find the fourth intercostal space by locating the **angle of Louis** (sternal angle), which is level with the second rib, and counting down.',
match('Match the lead to its position',[['V1','4th intercostal space, right sternal border'],['V2','4th intercostal space, left sternal border'],['V3','Midway between V2 and V4'],['V4','5th intercostal space, left midclavicular line'],['V5','Left anterior axillary line, level with V4'],['V6','Left midaxillary line, level with V4']]),
chk('Where is V4 placed?',['Fifth intercostal space at the midclavicular line','Fourth intercostal space to the right of the sternum','Fourth intercostal space to the left of the sternum','Left anterior axillary line'],'A HOSA sample question.')]],
['Obtaining a 12-lead EKG (Skill VI)',[
'In Skill VI (10 minutes), the competitor **reviews the test request**, greets the patient and identifies self, **provides privacy**, performs hand hygiene, **identifies the patient by ID bracelet and stated name and birthdate**, **explains the procedure and obtains consent**, assists the patient into a **supine position**, places the machine beside the bed and plugs it in, **turns it on and waits for the self-test**, and **enters the patient’s name and date of birth or scans the barcode**, confirming the correct patient. They **verbalize preparing the skin** by removing lotion or clipping hair if needed, **attach an electrode to each lead wire**, and **attach the electrodes in the correct positions**—V1, V2, V4, V3, V5, V6, then LA, LL, RA and RL—**avoiding bony areas, broken skin, scar tissue and skin over implanted devices**.',
'They ask the patient to **relax, stay still and breathe normally**, and when the tracing is clear (or the machine shows a green light), **print the EKG**. After the judge confirms it is usable, they **disconnect the lead wires without pulling the skin**, have the patient remove and dispose of the electrodes, perform hand hygiene, **verbalize informing unit staff**, **uploading the file to the medical record**, **documenting**, **following policy for reporting abnormalities** and **cleaning and storing the machine**, perform hand hygiene again and **maintain privacy throughout by exposing only what is necessary** (60 points; 70% is 42).',
order('12-lead EKG (HOSA Skill VI)',['Review the request; greet; privacy; hand hygiene; identify; consent','Position supine; plug in machine; self-test; enter patient data','Prepare skin; attach electrodes to lead wires','Place V1, V2, V4, V3, V5, V6','Place LA, LL, RA, RL avoiding bone, scars and devices','Patient relaxes and breathes normally; print clear tracing','Disconnect leads gently; remove electrodes','Inform staff, upload, document, report abnormalities, clean machine'])]],
['Artifacts and troubleshooting',[
'**Artifact** is interference that is not from the heart. **Somatic tremor** (wavy, jittery baseline) results from muscle movement, shivering or talking—keep the patient warm and relaxed with arms at the sides. **Wandering baseline** results from poor electrode contact, lotion or breathing movement—prepare skin and check electrodes. **AC (60-cycle) interference** produces a thick, regular baseline from nearby electrical equipment—unplug unnecessary devices and check grounding. **Broken or interrupted recordings** result from loose leads. **Reversed limb leads** (for example, RA and LA swapped) change the waveform direction in lead I; recheck placement before printing. Artifact can mimic dangerous rhythms, so always check the patient before calling an emergency.',
{t:'w',kind:'ecg',title:'Rhythms and artifacts',prompt:'Compare normal sinus rhythm with arrhythmias and artifacts. Which would you report immediately?'}]],
['Rhythms to recognize and report',[
'PCTs do not interpret EKGs for diagnosis, but they recognize when to alert the nurse. **Normal sinus rhythm** has a regular rate of 60–100 with a P wave before each QRS. **Sinus bradycardia** is below 60; **sinus tachycardia** above 100. **Atrial fibrillation** is irregularly irregular with no clear P waves. **Ventricular tachycardia** shows wide, rapid QRS complexes; **ventricular fibrillation** is chaotic with no recognizable complexes; **asystole** is a flat line. **Ventricular tachycardia, ventricular fibrillation and asystole** are emergencies—check the patient, call for help and start CPR if the patient is unresponsive and pulseless, as trained. Follow facility policy for reporting any abnormal tracing and **always assess the patient**, not just the monitor.',
kase('An alarming tracing',{a:{text:'During a 12-lead EKG, the screen shows a chaotic, wavy line. What do you do first?',choices:[['Check the patient—are they responsive and breathing?','b','Correct. Artifact can mimic ventricular fibrillation; the patient comes first.',true],['Print it and leave','b','Never assume—check the patient.',false]]},b:{text:'The patient is talking comfortably but shivering. What is the likely cause?',choices:[['Somatic tremor artifact; warm the patient and have them relax','c','Correct. Muscle movement causes artifact.',true],['Ventricular fibrillation','c','A talking patient is not in ventricular fibrillation.',false]]},c:{text:'With a blanket and calm breathing, a clear tracing prints. You document and inform the nurse.',end:true}})]],
["Telemetry and ambulatory monitoring",["**Telemetry** continuously monitors a patient’s heart rhythm through a small wireless transmitter connected to chest electrodes, displayed at a central station watched by monitor technicians or nurses. PCTs may apply telemetry electrodes (often in a 3- or 5-lead configuration), change batteries, respond to alarms for leads off and report rhythm changes. When an alarm sounds, always **check the patient first**. Patients on telemetry need to stay on the unit unless the transmitter range is confirmed.","Outpatients may wear a **Holter monitor** for 24–48 hours or an **event monitor** for longer periods to capture intermittent symptoms. Patients keep a **diary** of activities and symptoms, avoid getting the device wet and do not use electric blankets or magnets near it. Electrode sites are prepared like a 12-lead EKG, and skin is checked for irritation.",{"t":"w","kind":"match","title":"Match the device to its use","pairs":[["Telemetry","Continuous rhythm monitoring on a hospital unit"],["Holter monitor","Records every beat for 24–48 hours at home"],["Event monitor","Captures intermittent symptoms over weeks"],["12-lead EKG","Snapshot of 12 views of the heart"],["Patient diary","Links symptoms with recorded rhythms"],["Leads-off alarm","Check electrode contact and the patient"]]}]]
],takeaways:['Conduction: SA node → AV node → bundle of His → bundle branches → Purkinje fibers; P = atrial depolarization, QRS = ventricular depolarization, T = repolarization.','V1/V2: 4th intercostal space right/left of the sternum; V4: 5th intercostal space midclavicular; V3 between V2 and V4; V5 anterior axillary and V6 midaxillary level with V4.','Skill VI: privacy, ID, consent, supine, self-test, enter data, prep skin, place electrodes avoiding bone, scars and devices, still and breathing normally, print, disconnect gently, inform, upload, document, report; check the patient before trusting an alarming tracing.'],terms:[['Depolarization','Electrical activation of heart muscle.'],['Artifact','Interference on a tracing not caused by the heart.'],['Precordial leads','The six chest leads V1–V6.']],mcqs:[q('Where is V4 placed?','Fifth intercostal space at the midclavicular line','Fourth intercostal space right of the sternum','Fourth intercostal space left of the sternum','Left anterior axillary line','A HOSA sample question.'),q('How many electrodes are used for a 12-lead EKG?','10','12','6','4','Four limb and six chest electrodes.'),q('V1 is placed at the:','Fourth intercostal space, right sternal border','Fifth intercostal space, midclavicular line','Left midaxillary line','Right leg','V2 is at the left sternal border.'),q('The natural pacemaker of the heart is the:','SA node','AV node','Purkinje fibers','Bundle of His','It starts each beat.'),q('The QRS complex represents:','Ventricular depolarization','Atrial depolarization','Ventricular repolarization','Atrial repolarization','The T wave is repolarization.'),q('A wavy, jittery baseline from shivering is:','Somatic tremor artifact','AC interference','Asystole','Normal sinus rhythm','Warm and relax the patient.'),q('Electrodes should avoid:','Bony areas, broken skin, scars and implanted devices','Muscle tissue entirely','The chest','The limbs','A Skill VI step.'),q('Before printing, the patient should:','Relax, stay still and breathe normally','Talk to the technician','Hold their breath for a minute','Sit up and wave','Movement causes artifact.')],frq:{prompt:'Describe how to obtain a 12-lead EKG following HOSA Skill VI, including electrode placement and how you would handle a tracing that shows artifact.',rubric:[['Preparation and privacy',['request','privacy','identif','consent','supine','self-test','enter']],['Electrode placement',['v1','v2','v3','v4','v5','v6','intercostal','midclavicular','axillary']],['Recording and completion',['still','breathe','print','disconnect','upload','document','report','clean']],['Artifact handling',['artifact','tremor','check the patient','lotion','electrode','interference']]],model:'I would review the request, greet the patient, provide privacy, perform hand hygiene, identify the patient by the ID bracelet and stated name and birthdate, explain the test, obtain consent and help them lie supine. I would plug in the machine, let it complete its self-test and enter or scan the patient’s information, then prepare the skin by removing lotion or clipping hair if needed and attach electrodes to the lead wires. I would place V1 at the fourth intercostal space right of the sternum, V2 at the fourth intercostal space left of the sternum, V4 at the fifth intercostal space at the left midclavicular line, V3 midway between V2 and V4, V5 level with V4 at the left anterior axillary line and V6 level with V4 at the left midaxillary line, then the limb electrodes on the left arm, left leg, right arm and right leg, avoiding bony areas, broken skin, scars and implanted devices and exposing only what is needed. I would ask the patient to relax, stay still and breathe normally and print when the tracing is clear. If I saw artifact, I would first check the patient, then correct the cause—warming a shivering patient, checking electrode contact and skin prep, or moving electrical devices away—and repeat the tracing. Afterward I would disconnect the leads gently, have the electrodes removed, inform the unit staff, upload and document the EKG, report abnormalities per policy and clean and store the machine.'},sources:[RULES,MEDLINE_ECG,AHA_ARR]};
